 { In such rook endings with an extra pawn on the queenside, the location of both rooks play a key role. Despite the fact that Black's rook is ideally placed behind the pawn (more about this idea later on in this chapter) White wins rather easily, since he has avoided the worst position for his own rook - in front of the pawn - and defends it from the side. } 1. { -KEY- } b5 h5 2. Ke3 $1 { Andersson doesn't bother about losing a pawn or two on the kingside, and focuses on assisting his passed pawn with the king. } Rb2 3. Kd4 Rxg2 4. b6 Rb2 5. Kc5 Rc2+ 6. Kb5 ( 6. Kd6 { is a good alternative, as observed by Anunnaki. After } Rxh2 7. b7 ( 7. Rb5 $5 Rd2 { might leave White's king short of an ideal hideout. } ) 
Rb2 8. Kc6 { , the threat of Rb5 forces Black to } Rxb7 { , and after } 9. Kxb7 { , White wins comfortably by bringing back the king along c6-c5-d4 etc. } ) 
Rxh2 7. Rd4 $1 ( 7. Rd4 { enables a bridge in the event of } Rb2 8. Rb4 ) 
 ( { In any case, the simple } 7. b7 { would also have worked, since after } Rb2 8. Kc6 { the threat of another bridge by 9.Rb5 forces Black into } Rxb7 9. Kxb7 { when White wins easily by swiftly returning the King to the kingside. } ) 
 ( { Actually even } 7. Kc6 { or } 7. Ka6 { preparing 8.b7 would work as well, again relying on bridges along the 5th rank if necessary. } ) 
Re2 8. b7 Re8 9. Rc4 Kg7 10. Rc8 Re1 11. b8=Q Rb1+ 12. Kc4 
 1-0

