 { Sometimes in endings with bishops of the same color, an extra pawn is insufficient for victory, but here the fact that Black's d-pawn has made all the way to d3, together with White's compromised pawn structure (the g-pawn would have been better off on g2) makes a win probable. Nevertheless, Black has to play very accurately. } 1... { -KEY- } Kd4 { Intending 2...Ke3, a threat that White hurries to parry. } ( { The immediate } 1... Kc3 { would have failed because of } 2. Bf6 ) 
2. Bf2+ Kc3 { Thanks to his clever first move Black is now ready to push ...d3-d2. } 3. Bb6 d2 4. Bf2 Kd3 5. Bb6 Bf4 ( { A bridge with } 5... Be3 { is less efficient due to } 6. Ba5 ) 
6. Bf2 Be5 ( { Here, on the other hand, } 6... Be3 $5 { was quite interesting; e.g. } 7. Bg3 hxg4 $1 8. hxg4 Bd4 9. Bf4 Bc3 { when both } 10. Bg5 ( { and } 9... Be5 9. Bh6 { are met by } f6 { preparing ... g6-g5 and finishing off White's waiting moves. Instead, Smyslov plans to build the bridge on d4, so that the king can move instantly to e3 if White declines the exchange of bishops. } ) 
 ) 
7. Bg1 h4 $1 { Fixing a favourable structure. } ( { The immediate } 7... Bd4 { is less effective because of } 8. Bh2 $1 { when } Ke3 9. Bg1 Kxf3 10. Bxd4 { leads to a draw - Smyslov. } ) 
8. Bf2 Bc3 ( { Again } 8... Bd4 { is premature due to } 9. Bxh4 Ke3 10. Bg5 Kxf3 11. Kxd2 Kg3 12. Ke2 Kxh3 13. Kf3 { etc. } ) 
9. Bg1 ( { a) After } 9. Bxh4 Ke3 { Black's king invades decisively; } ) 
 ( { b) while } 9. f4 Ke4 10. f5 g5 11. Ke2 Bd4 $1 12. Bxd4 Kxd4 13. Kxd2 f6 { also wins for him - Smyslov. } ) 
Bd4 $1 { After some careful calculation of the ensuing pawn ending, Black finally builds his bridge. } 10. Bxd4 ( { In the event of } 10. Bh2 Ke3 11. Bg1 Kxf3 $1 12. Bxd4 Kg3 { , Black wins by one tempo; e.g. } 13. Kxd2 Kxh3 14. Bf2 g5 15. Be3 Kg2 $1 16. Bxg5 h3 17. Bf4 h2 18. Bxh2 Kxh2 19. Ke3 Kg3 { - Smyslov. } ) 
Kxd4 11. Kxd2 Ke5 12. Ke3 g5 $1 { Gaining the opposition. } ( { After } 12... g5 13. Ke2 Kf4 14. Kf2 f6 { the story is repeated, so White resigned. } ) 

 0-1
